From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 7778777 <7778777@7778777.online>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 17:16:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] tmpfs: keep mappings of an unlinked file working after its
 last close

Shared memory is commonly created, unlinked and mapped, and its file
descriptor closed: shm_open and shm_unlink, SHM_ANON, or Wayland clients'
buffers.  On tmpfs every later access to such a mapping faults.

When the last reference to an unlinked node goes away, diskfs_drop_node
truncates it to zero, and diskfs_truncate shrinks the default pager's
memory object to zero with it.  The mappings still reference that object,
and every page now lies beyond its end.

Leave the memory object's size alone in that case: diskfs_drop_node is
the only caller that truncates a node whose cache_id is already zero.  The
node's storage is still released, and the kernel frees the memory object
with its last mapping.
---
 tmpfs/node.c | 13 ++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tmpfs/node.c b/tmpfs/node.c
index ff6c28eb..b0ddbf74 100644
--- a/tmpfs/node.c
+++ b/tmpfs/node.c
@@ -74,8 +74,8 @@ diskfs_free_node (struct node *np, mode_t mode)
     {
     case DT_REG:
       if (np->dn->u.reg.memobj != MACH_PORT_NULL) {
-      /* XXX GNU Mach will terminate the object, and thus existing mappings
-       * will get SIGBUS.  */
+	/* Mappings of the file keep the memory object alive (see
+	   diskfs_truncate); only tmpfs's own references go here.  */
 	vm_deallocate (mach_task_self (), np->dn->u.reg.memref, 4096);
 	mach_port_deallocate (mach_task_self (), np->dn->u.reg.memobj);
       }	
@@ -457,7 +457,14 @@ diskfs_truncate (struct node *np, off_t size)
   off_t set_size = size;
   size = round_page (size);
 
-  if (np->dn->u.reg.memobj != MACH_PORT_NULL)
+  /* diskfs_drop_node truncates an unlinked file to zero once its last
+     reference is gone (diskfs_try_dropping_softrefs has cleared cache_id
+     by then).  The file may still be mapped, as shared memory usually is
+     after shm_unlink or unlink and close: do not shrink the memory object
+     then, or those mappings fault.  The kernel releases the object with
+     its last mapping.  */
+  if (np->dn->u.reg.memobj != MACH_PORT_NULL
+      && !(size == 0 && np->cache_id == 0))
     {
       error_t err = default_pager_object_set_size (np->dn->u.reg.memobj, set_size);
       if (err == MIG_BAD_ID)
-- 
2.56.0

